WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.720
Good to have you on fascinating frontiers, episode 111 for June 24th, 2026.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.140
Here's your space in science briefing, an asteroid impact that ended the dinosaurs

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:17.480
also sustained underground conditions for life 8 million years longer than earlier estimates.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:24.040
New dating of alteration minerals shows heated groundwater circulated for roughly 8 million

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:26.600
years after the impact at Chicksalub.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:33.040
The system created a subsurface environment with liquid water, chemical gradients and nutrients

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:39.800
long after surface conditions stabilized. This duration exceeds earlier 2 million year estimates

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:46.000
and widens the window for possible microbial colonization. Researchers will next search for

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.020
biosignatures in core samples from the crater peak ring. The 8 million year degeneration

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:57.500
stands out because it triples earlier estimates and greatly extends the period when liquid water

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.920
and chemical energy could have supported life beneath the crater floor.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:08.420
Now we turn from one impact altered world to galaxies reshaped by star formation with new

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:14.580
views of the cigar galaxy. The James Webb Space Telescope's infrared data added to Hubble

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:21.260
archival images now show both the dust obscured core and the extended plumes of ejected material

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:28.720
in the cigar galaxy. Rapid star formation triggered by a past merger is driving gas and dust outward,

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.480
which will eventually star future generations of stars. The combined data set reveals how feedback

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:41.680
from young stars disrupts the disc on short time scales relative to the galaxy's overall life.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:48.100
Follow-up observations will track how quickly the outflow clears channels through the remaining gas

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:55.540
reservoir. While M82 shows feedback in action, another Webb program has used infrared to count stars

00:01:55.540 --> 00:02:02.840
hidden behind dust in an edge-hawn spiral. Infrared observations pierce thick lanes of dust to map

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:09.360
the thin disc and bulge structure of an unnamed edge-hawn spiral. The census provides a rare

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:16.040
side-on census of stellar populations across the full radial extent. This view helps calibrate

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:20.920
models of how dust and stars are distributed in galaxies viewed at other angles.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.880
Future papers will compare the star counts against simulations of disc formation.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:31.580
If every visible star in the Milky Way were switched off tomorrow, the galaxy would still shine

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:36.900
brightly in infrared from the dust-concentrated stars we cannot see with ordinary telescopes.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:43.900
Dust grains absorb optical and ultraviolet photons and re-radiate the energy at longer wavelength

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:49.980
that pass straight through the same clouds. The James Webb Space Telescopes' detectors are tuned

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:55.760
to these wavelengths, allowing it to count individual stars in regions where visible light images

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:03.640
show only darkness. In one recent observation, the telescope recorded 16.5 million stars across a

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:08.880
single edge-on-disc, whose visible appearance is little more than a thin dark line.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:15.120
That number comes from resolving point sources down to roughly the mass of the sun at distances

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:21.380
of several million light-years. Yet even with these data, we still cannot say precisely how much

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:27.200
additional mass remains locked in objects too faint or too deeply buried for current instruments

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:33.720
to detect. The same infrared advantage that uncovers hidden stars is also letting astronomers watch

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:40.480
a much earlier galaxy clear its own surroundings. Ultraviolet light from clustered young stars in

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:49.880
MXD-FZ-4.4 is ionizing and dispersing neutral hydrogen, both inside and immediately around the

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:58.000
galaxy. The object existed when the universe was 1.4 billion years old. This is the earliest direct

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.900
evidence that such compact starbursts helped end the cosmic dark ages. Additional spectroscopy will

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:12.060
measure the exact fraction of ionizing photons escaping into intergalactic space. From galaxies

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:17.880
clearing space around them, we move to a rover choosing where to look next on an ancient Martian

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:24.320
lake bed. The rover's science team identified orbital texture differences and plans a short drive

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:30.760
toward a smoother patch for closer inspection. The new site may expose different mineral assemblages

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:36.520
than the rougher bedrock examined recently. Onboard instruments will measure chemistry and texture

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:42.460
to test whether the change records a shift in ancient lake or wind conditions. The next planning

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:48.580
cycle will decide whether to stop for contact science or continue toward higher strata. While curiosity

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:54.860
plans its next stop, we look back at how the Apollo crews once trained to read alien landscapes on

00:04:54.860 --> 00:05:00.940
Earth. Before their flights, crews practice sampling and traverse planning in the valley of 10,000

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:08.380
smokes, a landscape shaped by volcanic ash flows. The exercises built skills for rapid identification

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:14.560
of rock types and structures under time pressure. Lessons from those rehearsals shaped how astronauts

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:21.300
documented samples returned from the moon. Archival photos and audio from the training are now part

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:28.520
of a new public exhibit. From training on Earth to extreme environments in space, we examine one planet

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:34.500
pushed to atmospheric limits by its star. The planet's orbit keeps one hemisphere under continuous

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:41.420
high energy bombardment from its host star. Models show the resulting temperature contrast drive strong

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:47.760
winds that redistribute heat to the night side. The extreme conditions test the limits of atmospheric

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:54.200
circulation theories developed for cooler giants. On-going observations aim to measure how the

00:05:54.200 --> 00:06:00.940
upper atmosphere expands and escapes under this forcing. From extreme planetary environments to the

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:06.900
infrastructure that supports getting there, NASA's Inspector General has flagged capacity concerns

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:13.480
at Kennedy Space Center. An Inspector General report warns that aging infrastructure and growing

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:20.060
launch demand are straining the Florida spaceport. Multiple heavy lift vehicles now compete for pads,

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:26.840
processing facilities and range resources. The findings recommend near-term investments to avoid

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:33.220
bottlenecks that could delay Artemis and commercial missions. NASA is reviewing the recommendations for

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:39.540
the next budget cycle. While launch capacity is reviewed, Boeing has secured a major new satellite

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:46.080
contract. The award covers two next-generation military communication satellites after a competition

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:52.460
against Lockheed Martin. The spacecraft will provide protected wideband services for US and

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:58.260
allied forces. Work will build on Boeing's existing satellite production line in California.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:05.920
First launch is expected in the early 2030s. From new hardware contracts to the ongoing effort to

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:11.940
return an existing crude vehicle to flight, an uncrewed test flight remains in the testing and

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:18.760
analysis phase before any crude attempt. Internal schedules list a possible August window, yet

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:25.460
officials cautioned that technical issues could push the flight into 2027. The companies continue

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:31.780
parallel work on software and hardware fixes identified during prior attempts. A final readiness

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:38.760
review date has not been set. While Starliner works through fixes, SpaceX has flown a new microgravity

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:45.040
research capsule. The capsule performed controlled flight after deployment from Falcon 9 and will splash

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:52.240
down in the Pacific. The demo validates a design intended for routine, lower-cost access to microgravity

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:58.100
for experiments and manufacturing. Payload capacity and re-entry performance data will inform

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:04.100
future operational missions. Additional flights are planned once the vehicle completes post-flight

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:11.140
review. From US commercial demos to an Australian hypersonic test. The Australian company completed

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:17.360
its first hypersonic flight test as part of building defense-related launch capability. The vehicle

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:23.360
reached speeds and altitudes relevant to future responsive launch concepts. Data from the flight

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:29.820
will guide development of a larger orbital rocket. The test site offers proximity to existing range

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:35.440
infrastructure. From hardware tests to the people and teams preparing the next generation.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:42.500
Student engineers advanced with a project focused on aerospace systems design. The team will compete

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:49.180
against other universities in a final round later this year. Faculty advisors note the project

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:55.680
builds directly on coursework in propulsion and avionics. Results will contribute to ongoing NASA

00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:01.800
education initiatives. Embry Riddle Prescott advanced through multiple review stages with their

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:08.560
aerospace systems design entry. The entry integrates propulsion hardware testing with avionics software

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:14.840
validation in a single vehicle concept. Competition judges selected the team after evaluating

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:21.580
technical documentation and preliminary hardware demonstrations. The final round later this year will

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:26.900
require live demonstrations of integrated system performance under simulated mission constraints.

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:33.580
From student teams to the weekly rhythm of launches on the space coast. The latest manifest list

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:40.300
upcoming Falcon, Atlas and Ariane flights from Florida pads through the end of summer. Several national

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:46.660
security and commercial missions remain on track despite earlier delays. Range and weather constraints

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:52.460
continue to influence exact liftoff times. The schedule is refreshed weekly as vehicles move

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:59.040
through processing. Additional Falcon heavy lift missions and an Atlas 5 national security payload

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:05.700
are listed through August. Processing teams coordinate shared use of the eastern range radar and

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:11.980
telemetry assets for each vehicle. While the manifest is updated NASA has named a new chief information

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:18.980
officer. Gallagher previously acting CIO now leads the agency's full portfolio of information

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:25.080
technology systems and services. His appointment is effective immediately and follows several months

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:31.300
in the acting role. Priorities include modernizing legacy systems and supporting growing data demands

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:38.220
from science missions. The move centralizes oversight of cybersecurity and cloud infrastructure across

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:45.500
centers. Gallagher will also coordinate IT support for expanding commercial crew and science operations

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:52.180
at multiple field centers. Keep an eye on the next starliner readiness review as testing continues

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:58.300
through the summer. That's fascinating frontiers for today. If you enjoyed this, a rating or review

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:05.100
on Apple podcasts or Spotify really helps new listeners find the show. I'm Patrick in Vancouver,

